From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Fri, 12 Aug 1994 10:23:11 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-124] PPL: News from Russia

 I just spoke to a friend of mine who returned from Russia a few days ago.
She told me that amidst the generally chaotic state and inability of the
government and local authorities to provide social stability, unexpectedly
for many people, the role of the social stabilizer is often assumed by...
mafia.

  Those men who were committing random crimes and aimlessly terrorizing
the population and fighting each other just a couple of years ago, now
changed black leather for suits, are getting organized, establishing
(and respecting) spheres of influence.
  
  They still come to your business (*any* business, even if you sell
newspapers or groceries), and offer 'protection' for pay.
If you already have a 'protection agency' - called a 'roof', they don't
touch you, but take the 'roof''s address and talk to them. They may take
you away from your current agency, but if there's any violence, it's 
between them, and you don't see it.

   The prices of protection are getting reasonable, the relationships -
long-term.  The agencies are now really protecting businesses from local
thieves and vandals.  They also provide additional services, such as
business debt collection and legal representation.
You can now pay them money 'officially', by check, as they are locally
registered as Protection Agencies (yes, that's the term).

   By popular opinion, they often provide better and cheaper services than
local authorities (which often are just another bunch of criminals) and
the official police.

   In many cases, businessmen are looking for such an agency themselves,
trying to find a strong, stable and affordable 'roof'.

   In the non-business life though, people, except the rich few, are still
vulnerable and uncertain...

  I don't know where it all may develop, but it sure looks like spontaneous
formation of PPLs.

  Maybe I made a mistake in my search for freedom when I moved from
Russia to this increasingly statist country...  ;-)


  Sasha from Russia.

