From: Max More <more@chaph.usc.edu>
Date: Thu, 11 Aug 1994 21:43:51 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-8-127] EPIST: Objective & subjective

>From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
>Subject: [#94-8-114] PHIL: Logician, heal thyself

>> Fine.  How does it follow from this definition that knowledge of those
>> objects is impossible?  You may not think it's possible, but that 
conclusion
>> surely doesn't follow *by definition.*

>I just quoted you two mutually exclusive definitions.  Are you incredibly 
>dense, or just being willfully obtuse?  Does anyone else have a problem 
with
>this?  I'm puzzled, I really am.

Yes, I have a problem with it too.  I also have a problem with your lack of 
civility in responding to Ed.  Even if he wasn't formerly a professor of 
philosophy (and so just maybe has an idea what he's saying), you might 
extend him the benefit of the doubt and some courtesy.  (Funny how so 
often the proponents of subjectivism are the ones to decide someone is 
either stupid or wicked for not seeing things their way....)

Let me re-quote your two definitions then explain why they are not 
mutually exclusive:


> objective (adj.) - existing indcependent of mind: relating to an object
> as it is in itself or as distinguished from the consciousness of the
> subject

> subjective (adj.) - characteristing of or belinging to reality as
> perceived or known as opposed to reality as it is in itself or
> independent of mind

The definition of objective talks of how things are in themselves, 
regardless of how any subject perceives them to be.  This definition of 
subjective refers to reality-as-we-perceive/conceive-it.  (I find the 
definition of subjective a little slippery and tendentious because of the 
phrase "as opposed to reality".  If we can take that to mean "as 
distinguished from -- which is how my online dictionary more effectively 
phrases it -- then I have no objection.  If you really mean "opposed" as 
equivalent to "incompatible with" then you have indeed shown that, by 
definition, we cannot know reality as it is.  But then I would reject the 
definition in favor of the "as distinguished from" phrasing.)

Since we are subjects, we subjectively perceive reality.  However, you 
have _not_ shown that our perceptions of reaity cannot accord 
with/correspond to reality.  The fact that we (and _any_  thinking being 
must use their subjective means in order to make contact with reality does 
not mean that we thereby can never know reality as it is.  As Rand put it: 
On your view, we are blind _because_ we have eyes, we are deaf 
_because_ we have ears....

Until Ed says more I don't know whether I agree with the rest of what he 
says, but I do concur in holding that we _can_ have objective knowledge.  
If we cannot, then let's just throw out science.  I recommend you go and 
read the works of Karl Popper, especially "Objective Knowledge", or the 
new book I just posted about: David Miller's "Critical Rationalism".

Objective knowledge has nothing to do with certainty, so its possibility is 
distinct from our judgement as to the possibility of certainty.

>More properly, there are degrees of certainty between the two ends.  
> You can never prove "never", because you can't wait more than aleph-
> null years to find out.  Ditto for "always".

You might benefit from reading some material on critical rationalism.  
(You could start gently by reading my paper in the EXTRO 1 
Proceedings.)  Holding a belief with certainty, according to critical 
rationalists (I don't know Ed's view on this) does _not_ mean that you've 
proved anything, nor does it mean you claim you could never possibly 
revise the belief.  I am certain that I am more than two years old.  Yet (for 
the reasons you cited) I can never _prove_ this view.  I don't give the 
alternatives any credence, and I can hold my belief open to criticism while 
still being certain about it:  I don't seriously doubt it.

>Extropianism is a personal preference.  Objective knowledge is an object
>of faith.

No, objective knowledge is a matter of continually applying objective 
standards (themselves open to challenge and revision) to our theories.  It 
doesn't guarantee truth, but it does allow us to move beyond the confines 
of our subjective pereceptions.

>> >Certainty is possible...
>> 
>> Okay, how?  

>You deleted the rest of the sentence which causes it to make sense, which
>is "...if you are willing to ignore the existence of future events which
>would disprove your certainty." (paraphrase)  Talk about selective 
quoting...

I think you opened yourself to it by failing to distinguish psychological 
from epistemic certainty.  Apparently you meant the former, while Ed 
reasonably (given the context) thought you meant the latter.

Onward!

Max More	more@usc.edu	more@extropy.org


