To add another book in the PCR framework that just came out: 
 
CRITICAL RATIONALISM: A RESTATEMENT AND DEFENCE 
David Miller 
(Open Court, Chicago, 1994) 
 
I've only glanced through it so far, but it appears to be a solid and quite  
technical examination Popper's view of knowledge and developments of  
it such as PCR (though Miller prefers CCR -- comprehensively critical  
rationalism.  It seems that Miller agress with Bartley, though says that  
some of Bartley's replies to critics were weak.  Miller claims to provide  
more effective responses. 
 
The chapter titles: 
1. Conjectural Knowledge 
2. Popper's Solution of the Problem of Induction 
3. A Critique of Good Reasons 
4. Comprehensively Critical Rationalism: An Assessment 
5. Hume: Bacon = Godel: Hilbert 
6. Three Lost Labors of Deductivism 
7. On the Maximization of Expected Futility [not a typo] 
8. Diverging Distributions 
9. Objective Probabilities 
10. Truth, Truthlikeness, Approximate Truth 
11. Impartial Truth 
 
