Date: Fri, 12 Aug 94 12:24:58 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-133] PHIL: Logician, heal thyself

grigsby@agames.com (Thu, Aug 11, 1994 1:36 AM EST) writes:

>I just quoted you two mutually exclusive definitions.  Are you >incredibly 
>dense, or just being willfully obtuse?  Does anyone else have >a problem
with
>this?  I'm puzzled, I really am.

Look, questioning people's intelligence or motives may be lots of fun, and
you may find it very satisfying emotionally, but it doesn't advance the
argument at all.  

There is in fact no problem about having knowledge of objects that exist
independent of consciousness.  Regarding such a thing as impossible would be
like thinking that a camera can't take pictures of objects outside itself
because the film, after all, is inside the camera whereas the objects are
outside it.  But in fact the camera can and does take pictures of external
objects and the mind can and does know objects that exist external to and
independently of itself.  This is what the senses do: they tell us about
objects that exist outside of us.    

>How can you prove that they [some statements] are more than assumptions?  
>This is the core of
>my challenge, which is that _you cannot prove the existence >of objective
>reality through your subjective perception_.

You can prove that some statements are more than assumptions by looking at
the objective evidence that exists for those statements, in the form of
sense-experience, experiment, and other facts and data.  Saying that "you
cannot prove the existence of objective reality through your subjective
perception" begs the question: it assumes that the only thing available to us
is "subjective perception," which is false.  

>You
>can never prove "never", because you can't wait more than >aleph-null years
>to find out.  Ditto for "always".

Well, then, why do you make the claim "you cannot prove the existence of
objective reality through your subjective perception"?  Doesn't that imply
you have gone through an infinite amount of evidence?  The fact is, it
doesn't take an infinite amount of evidence in order to establish a claim as
true, even when the claim pertains to an infinite number of objects, for
example, the real numbers.  

>I challenge you to show me _one piece_ of objective knowledge.

Okay: Most of chemistry, physics, astronomy, and large portions of molecular
biology, for starters.  For the pieces of objective evidence involved, they
exist by the ton in libraries, laboratories, and other such places.  

>> BTW, 1+1 = 2 is true even in "foreign" languages.

>It's meaningless in a language without Arabic numerals.

True enough, but what does that prove?  The claim that "Nothing can be known
for certain" is completely unintelligible in Swahili, but does that suffice
to invalidate the claim?  The truth-value of a meaningless assertion cannot
be assessed one way or the other.   In languages that do have Arabic
numerals, and in which the equation is a meaningful assertion, the statement
1+1=2 is an objectively true statement.  

>> And it could not be a
>> statement in binary, because binary does not admit of the numeral 2.  

>...therefore placing a conditional upon your supposed >"objective" truth!
>There are a lot more -- assuming linear space, assuming that >we're not
>counting levels of infinity, ... -- but you didn't mention that, >did you?

I don't understand this at all, sorry.  Objective truths can be conditional,
without any problem, for example: "For all real numbers, if x is greater than
y and if y is greater than z, then x is greater than z."     

>My thought for this discussion:  Those who insist most >strongly in the
>existence of objective reality are the most interested in >getting you
>to adopt their subjective view

Other than for the viewpoint that objective reality exists and that it's
knowable through reason, observation, and experiment, there is nothing I have
at stake here.  In my own experience, objectivists, relativists, nihilists,
theists and athiests, etc., and so on--all of them are equally fond of
getting other people to adopt their own perspectives.  I guess this is a
human trait, and one needn't find any evil motives here.  

