From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Fri, 12 Aug 94 12:56:55 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-134] Silicon Brain

John Schooley quotes Science News-

> >De Garis calls this approach 'evolutionary engineering'. The neural
> >net grows when cellular automata send 'growth signals' to each other,
> >then connect via 'synapses'...

If you've every seen Chris Langton's loop-with-a-hook shaped cellular
automaton, this is a variation.  Both are like Conway's game of Life,
where each cell on grid is playing by the same rules, only
Langton's and De Garis' setups have more rules and more states.

Langton seeds his grid with a sort of hollow letter Q, where
the outer layers are in a "skin" state, and--this is the interesting
part--the middle layer of the tube contains a string of instructions 
propogating around the loop.  There's a fork in the tube (the tail on
the Q) where the instructions duplicate and go both around
the loop again, and down the tail.  When the instructions get to the tip 
of the tail, they're interpreted and tell the tip which way to grow in the 
next step.  The instructions are a combination of "forward," "fork," and
"turn left" instructions that form a letter Q...  At some point the
umbilical from the parent is cut and the parent starts another child
while the child is starting a grandchild.  This ends up populating the 
plane with close-packed Q's (or backwards small g's).

DeGaris' thing seems to use a similar method, except that after
the grid gets filled with these tubes, they are used to propogate
nerve pulses instead of growth instructions.  Plus he has "synapses" 
form where the tubes collide.  I don't know more details.

DeGaris' idea seems to be that since this can be run in an array of
identical simple units (a cellular automaton) you can easily make
a specialized chip for it and then wire lots of the chips together...

-fnerd
